WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.380
Secure SCP and rsync use in Bash is a repeatable engineering habit.

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:08.120
Start with SSH trust.

3
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:15.580
Verify hosts, manage known-hosts data deliberately, and avoid casual trust bypasses in unattended jobs.

4
00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:21.920
Then use dedicated transfer identities with clear purpose, scope, ownership, and lifecycle.

5
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.160
Choose the transfer tool based on the workflow.

6
00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:31.260
Simple copy, controlled file-transfer interface, and incremental synchronization are different jobs.

7
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:39.760
Understand metadata, comparison behavior, delete behavior, and recovery needs before treating a command as production automation.

8
00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.400
Keep Bash invocation fixed and reviewable.

9
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:56.380
Use arrays for option and operand lists, quote values that should remain literal, and validate hosts, users, ports, source paths, destination paths, and relevant option values before execution.

10
00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.000
Avoid command strings and unnecessary re-parsing.

11
00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:03.240
Validate transfer scope before data moves.

12
00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:10.620
Reject empty paths, unsupported targets, unexpected source types, and destinations outside approved locations.

13
00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:16.640
Review permissions, ownership, metadata preservation, symlink behavior, and directory boundaries.

14
00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.700
Verify important post-transfer expectations.

15
00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:24.460
Use dry runs and guardrails for high-impact rsync workflows.

16
00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:35.280
Preview risky synchronization, protect last-known-good data where practical, and treat delete or overwrite behavior as a deliberate production decision rather than an option copied from another script.

17
00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:41.800
Finally, check results, log safely, monitor unusual outcomes, and review over time.

18
00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:45.420
Secure transfer automation is not a one-time script.

19
00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:54.000
It is an operational control that depends on keys, accounts, paths, options, destinations, logs, monitoring, and periodic review.
